---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import ReadyHandoff from '../components/ReadyHandoff.astro';
import ReadyProgress from '../components/ReadyProgress.astro';
import { ArrowRight, ArrowLeft, Check, BookOpen, Copy, ChevronDown } from '@lucide/astro';
const lessons = [
 {id:'workspace', title:'Meet your agent', result:'A folder your agent can actually inspect'},
 {id:'ready', title:'Prepare & prove', result:'Your own starter, running over HTTPS'},
 {id:'brief', title:'Make it yours', result:'A portable brief for a small first change'},
 {id:'publish', title:'Check the live change', result:'The greeting you chose, on your actual site'},
 {id:'compose', title:'Connect two apps', result:'Understand how a photo becomes a featured card'},
 {id:'content', title:'Shape your content', result:'Try fields and forms before implementation'},
 {id:'quality', title:'Make it feel good', result:'A usable page on a phone and a keyboard'},
 {id:'own', title:'Keep control', result:'Source, evidence and a clear next step'},
];
const rationale = {
 workspace:{title:'Why give the agent project context?',benefit:'A short brief and project instructions give future tasks the same starting point: who the app serves, what matters and how to check the result. You spend less time explaining the project again.',when:'Use an agent for a direct app, a reusable mini-app or an existing project. Composer is optional; useful project guidance carries across all three.',tradeoff:'Instructions and skills need to match the current project. Too much stale guidance can obscure the actual task; a file or installed skill does not prove the agent used it.'},
 ready:{title:'Why prove setup before building?',benefit:'A small deployed starter finds account, source and publishing problems before they become tangled with a larger app. Get Ready can also inspect an existing setup and reuse what already works.',when:'Start here if you do not yet know that your agent can publish to the intended account. If that environment is already verified, recheck what changed instead of reinstalling everything.',tradeoff:'A Hello page proves a narrow path. A different app, database, private login or domain still needs its own checks. Your starter’s private source repository does not make its web page private.'},
 brief:{title:'Why write down the outcome?',benefit:'A concrete brief makes the result reviewable: a visitor should be able to do a particular thing, with content and behaviour you can recognise. A portable specification makes those decisions easier to carry between tasks.',when:'Use Init when its supported settings fit. For an original standalone app, give the agent a plain-language brief and let it choose the smallest suitable implementation.',tradeoff:'A template gives you a working starting shape but limits what its existing settings express. A custom build gives more control and leaves more implementation and maintenance to your project.'},
 publish:{title:'Why check the released app?',benefit:'People use the deployed page, so check the intended address and main action after release. Keeping the source and deployed identity together makes later diagnosis and rollback clearer.',when:'Do this after a code release, configuration change, domain move or migration. A content-only publish also deserves a check that the intended public content appeared.',tradeoff:'Check the changed journey and important dependencies. A health endpoint or green build is useful evidence, but neither proves every action works. Larger changes need broader verification.'},
 compose:{title:'Why use mini-apps at all?',benefit:'A mini-app is a focused application with its own source and a declared interface. Reusing a reviewed photo collector or curated display can save you rebuilding that feature; composition connects them when they need to share data or actions.',when:'Choose this when an existing feature fits, has a compatible interface and benefits from separate ownership or reuse. One coherent app with tightly coupled behaviour may be simpler to build directly.',tradeoff:'Separate apps add contracts, version compatibility, deployment configuration and dependency failures to manage. Independent code does not guarantee independent data or uptime. You can use each app on its own.'},
 content:{title:'When is a CMS worth adding?',benefit:'EmDash gives editors a place to manage structured content, media and publication. Astro supplies the page layer. A model helps decide which information people enter and how the app uses it.',when:'Add a CMS when people need to edit content without changing code, or need editorial workflows. A small static page can stay in Astro; custom app behaviour can live in a Worker.',tradeoff:'A CMS adds a database, permissions, migrations, media handling and recovery work. Model previews express intent; required rules and access still need checking in the implemented app.'},
 quality:{title:'Why check the human experience?',benefit:'Clear words, readable layouts and predictable controls help people complete the task without learning your implementation. Watching someone use the app reveals confusion a code check can miss.',when:'Check the main journey early, then again when navigation, forms, content or layout change. Add a focused design or writing skill when it helps solve an observed problem.',tradeoff:'Start with the task people need to complete. Extra animation, generic polish or a large tool pack can add cost without improving that task. Judge the rendered result and actual use.'},
 own:{title:'What will this app need after launch?',benefit:'Source ownership, a clear update path and a tested recovery procedure help you keep using the app when code, data or account access goes wrong. The right level depends on what people rely on it for.',when:'Decide before accepting valuable content or making the app important to someone else. Revisit before risky migrations and when the consequences of downtime or lost data change.',tradeoff:'Backups, retention, monitoring and redundant deployments require attention and can cost money. Choose protection for a named failure and test it; adding more services alone does not establish resilience.'},
};
const checks = {
 workspace:['I can point to the folder my agent inspected.','The agent described what exists and what is missing.'],
 ready:['My agent checked my actual tools and account access.','I opened my own HTTPS starter and the agent matched its source.'],
 brief:['I saved a Hello brief and checked the title, introduction and theme.','I downloaded the spec and can find it on my device.'],
 publish:['The intended greeting appears on my live address after a refresh.','The agent reported the deployed source and checked the actual hostname.'],
 compose:['I opened a featured photo and followed it to its source entry.','I can explain who approves a photo and why hiding it affects both views.'],
 content:['I tried a sample form and inspected its exported model.','I know that a preview still needs implementation and live checks.'],
 quality:['I checked my app at a phone width and used its keyboard controls.','I asked someone to try its main action without coaching.'],
 own:['I know where my source and release evidence live.','I know who can update the app and what recovery has actually been checked.'],
};
const questions = [
 {question:'Does opening this tutorial connect your accounts?',answers:['No. My agent checks the selected workspace and access.','Yes. Opening the page completes setup.'],correct:0,why:'This page teaches the journey. Account connections happen through your authorised agent and official providers.'},
 {question:'What establishes that your starter is ready?',answers:['Copying the setup request.','Fresh checks of my environment and the intended source at my own HTTPS address.'],correct:1,why:'A copied request or another person’s working reference does not check your environment.'},
 {question:'What does Save and preview in Init do?',answers:['It saves a local draft for review.','It deploys my app to Cloudflare.'],correct:0,why:'The agent still needs to review, implement and release the saved specification.'},
 {question:'A build passed. What comes next?',answers:['Call the live app verified.','Deploy through the app’s release path, then check the hostname, source and behaviour.'],correct:1,why:'A build checks the source locally. Live verification checks what people actually receive.'},
 {question:'Who controls whether a featured photo is public?',answers:['Its source guestbook controls current approval and visibility.','The showcase owns a permanent public copy.'],correct:0,why:'The showcase resolves current source eligibility. Previously downloaded copies cannot be recalled.'},
 {question:'Does a downloaded content model make a working customer app?',answers:['Yes. Exporting it finishes the app.','No. It gives the agent a model to implement and verify.'],correct:1,why:'Fields and form previews express intent. They do not create a deployed customer application.'},
 {question:'What best checks whether a page is easy to use?',answers:['A real person completing its main action, plus phone and keyboard checks.','The number of design tools installed.'],correct:0,why:'Tools can help. Observed behaviour shows whether people can complete the task.'},
 {question:'Does restoring older code restore deleted photos?',answers:['Yes. Code history is a full data backup.','No. Code rollback and data recovery are separate.'],correct:1,why:'Recovery must preserve current privacy and use an explicitly checked data recovery path.'},
];
---
<Base title="Learn App Composer, one working step at a time" description="An interactive, plain-language App Composer tutorial. Follow your agent from first setup to a changed live app, explore composition and forms, and check the result at every stage.">
 <div class="learn-heading">
  <div><h1>Build something.<br/>Understand each step.</h1><p class="lede">Learn what each step gives you, when it helps and what you take on. Follow your agent to a working app, choose reusable pieces when they fit, or build directly with your preferred stack.</p></div>
  <div class="learn-start"><a class="button" href="#workspace" data-start>Start the tutorial <ArrowRight size={18} aria-hidden="true"/></a><p class="small">No app idea needed. Browse any lesson before setting up.</p></div>
 </div>
 <p class="learn-purpose"><strong>What does Composer add?</strong> Environment checks, reusable setup handoffs, portable app briefs and working composition examples in one place. Use the parts that help. Your own app can stay independent of Composer, with an agent building directly on Workers, Astro and optional EmDash.</p>
 <div class="learn-layout" data-tutorial>
  <aside class="lesson-index">
   <h2>Your route</h2>
   <nav aria-label="Tutorial lessons"><ol>{lessons.map((lesson,index)=><li><a href={'#'+lesson.id} data-lesson-link={lesson.id}><span class="lesson-stop" aria-hidden="true">{index+1}</span><span>{lesson.title}<small data-lesson-state={lesson.id} hidden></small></span></a></li>)}</ol></nav>
   <details class="learning-progress"><summary>Learning notes <span data-notes-count>0/16</span><ChevronDown size={16} aria-hidden="true"/></summary><p data-progress-count>0 of 16 observations noted</p><progress value="0" max="16" aria-label="Self-reported tutorial observations"></progress><p class="small">Your learning notes, saved on this device. These are separate from verified setup results.</p><p class="small" data-storage-status role="status"></p><button class="secondary" data-clear-progress hidden>Clear my learning notes</button><div data-clear-confirm hidden><p>Remove only this tutorial’s saved observations and return to lesson 1?</p><button data-confirm-clear>Clear notes</button><button class="secondary" data-cancel-clear>Keep notes</button></div></details>
  </aside>
  <div class="lesson-content">
   <div class="lesson-toolbar" data-lesson-toolbar>
    <p class="lesson-context" data-lesson-context>Follow along at your own pace · reviewed 14 October 2026</p>
    <select data-lesson-picker aria-label="Choose a lesson" disabled hidden>{lessons.map((lesson,index)=><option value={lesson.id}>{index+1}. {lesson.title}</option>)}</select>
    <a class="button secondary" data-lesson-previous href="#workspace" hidden><ArrowLeft size={18} aria-hidden="true"/><span>Previous</span></a>
    <a class="button secondary" data-lesson-next href="#ready" hidden><span>Next</span><ArrowRight size={18} aria-hidden="true"/></a>
   </div>
   {lessons.map((lesson,index)=><article id={lesson.id} class="lesson" data-lesson={lesson.id} aria-labelledby={lesson.id+'-title'}>
    <div class="lesson-heading"><span class="lesson-number" aria-hidden="true">{index+1}</span><div><h2 id={lesson.id+'-title'} tabindex="-1">{lesson.title}</h2><p>{lesson.result}</p></div></div>
    <section class="lesson-rationale" aria-labelledby={lesson.id+'-why'}>
     <h3 id={lesson.id+'-why'}>{rationale[lesson.id as keyof typeof rationale].title}</h3>
     <p>{rationale[lesson.id as keyof typeof rationale].benefit}</p>
     <dl><div><dt>Useful when</dt><dd>{rationale[lesson.id as keyof typeof rationale].when}</dd></div><div><dt>The trade-off</dt><dd>{rationale[lesson.id as keyof typeof rationale].tradeoff}</dd></div></dl>
    </section>

    {lesson.id==='workspace'&&<>
     <p>You decide what you want to make. Your coding agent works in your project folder: it inspects files, handles routine setup, builds and checks the result. You handle account sign-in and decisions about permissions or spending.</p>
     <p class="small">You can read this guide on any device. Automated starter setup is currently verified with Codex on an Apple Silicon Mac with Node 22 and Git. Ask your agent to check support first on another setup.</p>
     <h3>Try this</h3><ol><li>Open your coding agent and select a project folder. An empty folder is fine.</li><li>Ask it to inspect the folder before changing anything. If it contains work already, ask it to preserve that work.</li><li>Read its response: it should identify the actual folder, available tools and the next useful step.</li></ol>
     <div class="agent-request"><h3>Ask your agent</h3><p data-agent-prompt="workspace">Inspect this selected project folder without changing it. Explain what is already here, what tools you can actually use and the smallest next step for App Composer Get Ready. Preserve existing work. Do not install, create repositories, change accounts or deploy during this inspection. Keep credentials and private files out of the response.</p><button class="secondary" data-copy-prompt="workspace" hidden><Copy size={16} aria-hidden="true"/>Copy inspection request</button><p role="status" data-copy-status="workspace"></p></div>
     <h3>What a useful result looks like</h3><p>The agent can explain what it inspected and distinguish a missing tool from a missing login. “Everything looks good” without those checks is a reason to ask for the evidence.</p>
     <h3>Give future tasks a useful starting point</h3>
     <p><code>AGENTS.md</code> holds project-specific working instructions: purpose, real build commands, what to preserve and how to verify a change. A <strong>skill</strong> holds a reusable workflow, often in a folder containing <code>SKILL.md</code>. A <strong>tool or MCP connection</strong> gives the agent an actual capability. They solve different problems; instructions alone do not connect accounts or enforce permissions.</p>
     <ul><li><strong>Describe the outcome.</strong> Name the audience, main task, desired feel and what is out of scope. Tell the agent about an existing app or preferred stack.</li><li><strong>Inspect before adding.</strong> Preserve existing instructions and skills. Keep project rules short and use the commands and release path that actually exist.</li><li><strong>Add only relevant workflows.</strong> Design and copy help for public pages; official EmDash guidance for CMS work; diagramming when a relationship needs explaining. Check trusted sources, versions and installation scope.</li><li><strong>Verify use.</strong> Ask which instructions the selected agent loaded. For each required skill, distinguish present, discoverable and exercised on a relevant task. Keep account access and live deployment checks separate.</li></ul>
     <div class="agent-request"><h3>Prepare the guidance for your project</h3><p class="small">Replace the bracketed phrases with your idea. You can also describe it in your own words.</p><p data-agent-prompt="guidance">I want to build [what people should be able to do] for [the audience], with [the feel and constraints I care about]. Inspect this selected project and preserve its existing work. Prepare concise project instructions from the actual source: purpose, supported commands, boundaries and how to check the intended result. Reconcile existing AGENTS.md and product/design guidance rather than replacing it blindly. Recommend the smallest relevant skill/tool set, check trusted sources and compatibility, and verify what this agent can discover and use. Identify missing capabilities honestly. Handle routine local preparation within existing authority; ask for unavoidable sign-in, spending or consequential permissions. Keep credentials out of files and chat.</p><button class="secondary" data-copy-prompt="guidance" hidden><Copy size={16} aria-hidden="true"/>Copy project-guidance request</button><p role="status" data-copy-status="guidance"></p></div>
     <p class="small">Get Ready does not yet install and exercise every relevant skill in every intended app project. This is work for the selected agent to inspect and prove. Official OpenAI documentation explains <a href="https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/agent-configuration/agents-md">project instructions</a> and <a href="https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/build-skills">skills</a>; other agents can use different conventions.</p>
     <details><summary>New to agents, GitHub or Cloudflare?</summary><dl><dt>Coding agent</dt><dd>The assistant doing technical work in your selected folder.</dd><dt>GitHub repository</dt><dd>The home for your app’s source and change history.</dd><dt>Cloudflare Worker</dt><dd>The deployed application that serves your site.</dd><dt>HTTPS</dt><dd>An encrypted connection to a web address. We also check that it serves the intended app.</dd></dl><p>The current supported setup path uses Codex on an Apple Silicon Mac with Node 22 and Git available. Other agents and systems need separate verification. <a href="/ready/guide#first-step">Use the maintained first-setup instructions</a> if you need help opening the agent.</p></details>
    </>}

    {lesson.id==='ready'&&<>
     <p>Now hand over preparation. Get Ready uses one shared engine to check tools, GitHub source, Cloudflare access and a deployed starter. For a starter with a private source repository, begin with a temporary workers.dev address in an existing authorised Cloudflare account; you can consider a domain later.</p>
     <h3>Try this</h3><ol><li>Open <a href="/ready" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Get Ready in another tab</a>. Keep this lesson open while you choose your first address.</li><li>Copy the setup request there, paste it into the agent for your folder and send it.</li><li>If the agent identifies a login, finish only that step on the provider’s official screen. Tell the agent when you are ready so it can recheck.</li><li>Open the HTTPS address it gives you. Ask it to match the live app to the intended repository and deployed source.</li></ol>
     <details><summary>Copy a setup, inspection or resume request here</summary><ReadyHandoff/></details>
     <h3>See actual check results</h3><p>Ask the agent for its redacted Get Ready JSON export, then open it below. This reuses Get Ready’s report viewer and freshness checks. It reads the file locally. The private receipt is a different file and should stay private.</p><ReadyProgress/>
     <details><summary>If a check stops</summary><p>For sign-in or account access, use the <a href="/ready/guide#account-sign-in">maintained account instructions</a>. For a missing tool, let the agent inspect and repair the supported setup. A provider outage needs a bounded retry; a changed target needs a fresh check. Preserve partial work instead of restarting blindly.</p><p>First-account creation, broader agent/OS coverage and new-domain bootstrap remain incomplete. Check <a href="/release">current release evidence</a> before choosing an unsupported path. A domain purchase or a new billing commitment needs your approval.</p></details>
     <details><summary>Why start without a domain?</summary><p>A workers.dev address lets you test the supported private starter before moving DNS or buying a name. It still needs your intended Cloudflare account and verified deployment access. <a href="https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/configuration/routing/workers-dev/">Cloudflare explains workers.dev addresses</a>.</p></details>
    </>}

    {lesson.id==='brief'&&<>
     <p>After your starter is verified, make one small change. For this first pass, configure a Hello page. Keeping the change small makes it easy to see whether the full journey works.</p>
     <h3>Try this</h3><ol><li>Open <a href="/init" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Init in another tab</a>. Under <strong>Apps</strong>, choose <strong>Hello World reference</strong>.</li><li>Try this sample: title <strong>My first corner</strong>, introduction <strong>A small place to start making things.</strong>, theme <strong>Midnight</strong>. Or write your own.</li><li>For your verified private workers.dev starter, choose <strong>A temporary Cloudflare address</strong>. It describes your existing verified destination.</li><li>Select <strong>Save and preview</strong>. Inspect the words and theme, then use <strong>Download spec</strong> and <strong>Copy to Codex</strong>.</li></ol>
     <h3>Check the draft before sending it</h3><p>The portable specification should contain the title, introduction, theme and destination intent you selected. Your draft stays on this device. Saving and previewing it does not change a live app.</p>
     <details><summary>What is a specification?</summary><p>A specification is a structured description of the app you want. It gives your agent a concrete starting point you can review and carry to another project. It contains configuration, never passwords or secret keys.</p><p><a href="/explore">Decision Explorer</a> helps compare meaningful choices without executing them. You can change your mind there without creating an app.</p></details>
     <details><summary>I want to start with photos instead</summary><p>Init also exposes Guestbook and Showcase configuration. Explore those previews freely. The automated owned-starter update in this tutorial is the three-field Hello path; another customer app needs its own implementation and release verification. Lesson 5 shows the existing composed photo pair without asking you to deploy it first.</p></details>
    </>}

    {lesson.id==='publish'&&<>
     <p>Give your downloaded Hello specification and copied handoff to the agent in your verified starter folder. It should review the requested changes, follow the starter’s release rules and check the result online.</p>
     <h3>Try this</h3><ol><li>Ask the agent to confirm that the specification targets your already verified app and source.</li><li>Let it apply the supported title, introduction and theme changes, run the required checks, then commit and push the intended source.</li><li>After the authorised deployment, open your app’s exact HTTPS address and refresh. Compare the greeting and theme with your specification.</li><li>Ask the agent for the actual source revision, deployed identity and behaviour it checked. Keep that evidence with your project.</li></ol>
     <div class="agent-request"><h3>Ask for the result</h3><p data-agent-prompt="publish">Check the outcome of this Hello update. Compare the supplied specification with my intended repository and current live HTTPS hostname. Report the actual source revision, deployed identity, title, introduction and theme you verified. Separate local build results from live behaviour. If the update has not been deployed, name the exact remaining step and required authority; do not report it as complete.</p><button class="secondary" data-copy-prompt="publish" hidden><Copy size={16} aria-hidden="true"/>Copy verification request</button><p role="status" data-copy-status="publish"></p></div>
     <h3>If you still see the old greeting</h3><p>Confirm the address first. Ask the agent to compare the live source with the new commit and investigate the release or cache. A working old page does not establish that the update succeeded. Keep the previous working release while the agent diagnoses the difference.</p>
     <details><summary>Understand the evidence</summary><dl><dt>Build passed</dt><dd>The changed source can build locally.</dd><dt>Deployed</dt><dd>The provider accepted a release.</dd><dt>Live verified</dt><dd>The intended hostname serves the expected source and behaviour.</dd><dt>You tried it</dt><dd>You saw the result and used the page on your own device.</dd></dl><p>These answer different questions. <a href="/docs#hello-init">Read the supported Hello update path</a>.</p></details>
    </>}

    {lesson.id==='compose'&&<>
     <h3>You can build one app directly</h3>
     <p>Yes: ask your agent to build your own app on Workers with Astro and, if you need editable content, EmDash. You can choose your layout, data model and behaviour without adopting Composer’s mini-app contracts. You can also use the independent Get Ready capability without composing apps. <a href="https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/deploy/cloudflare/">Astro documents direct Cloudflare deployment</a>; <a href="https://docs.emdashcms.com/getting-started/">EmDash documents its own site setup</a>.</p>
     <table class="approach-table"><caption>Choose by the job you need to do</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Approach</th><th scope="col">Benefit and responsibility</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Build one app directly</th><td><p>Good for a distinctive workflow or tightly connected features. Your agent can design one coherent project without composition contracts.</p><p class="small">Your project owns the feature implementation, permissions, updates and recovery. You can still reuse ordinary libraries.</p></td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Use a mini-app on its own</th><td><p>Good when a reviewed existing app already solves the problem. Guestbook and Showcase each have their own deployment and source.</p><p class="small">Check licence, configuration fit, current behaviour and maintenance. Custom changes may require more work than the settings expose.</p></td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Compose mini-apps</th><td><p>Good when useful independent features need to exchange approved data or actions, as the photo collector and showcase do.</p><p class="small">Check both apps and the connection: interface versions, access, source visibility, compatible updates and failure handling. Shared infrastructure can still fail together.</p></td></tr>
     </tbody></table>
     <p><strong>A sensible starting point:</strong> choose one app unless reuse or an independently maintained feature solves a concrete need. Splitting a small app into more parts is not automatically easier, cheaper or more reliable.</p>
     <details><summary>A direct-build request for your agent</summary><p class="small">Replace the bracketed phrases. This starts a project discussion and implementation through that project’s own rules; it is not a supported automated customer-provisioning operation in Composer.</p><p data-agent-prompt="direct">Build a standalone app for [audience] that lets people [main task]. I want [content, design and constraints]. Inspect and preserve the selected project first. Recommend the simplest suitable structure: Astro for pages, Workers for required server behaviour, and EmDash only if editorial content needs it. Explain the benefits and trade-offs before adding independently deployed parts. Prepare concise project guidance and relevant skills, then implement a small complete journey. Use the intended authorised account and destination, existing release gates and live verification. Before valuable data is accepted, explain the backup, restore, access and cost requirements. Ask for human-only decisions or permissions; do not create a new paid commitment.</p><button class="secondary" data-copy-prompt="direct" hidden><Copy size={16} aria-hidden="true"/>Copy standalone-app request</button><p role="status" data-copy-status="direct"></p></details>
     <h3>See where composition earns its place</h3>
     <p>Composition connects independently maintained apps through data and actions. Here / there owns contributed photos and their approval. Top shelf curates approved photos into a showcase and links each one back to its original.</p>
     <h3>Try the public path</h3><ol><li>Open the <a href="/showcase/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">composed showcase in another tab</a>.</li><li>Choose a featured photo and open its original contribution.</li><li>Check that the guestbook entry and showcase card refer to the same item.</li><li>Return to the tutorial and read the visibility check below.</li></ol>
     <figure class="composition-example"><img src="/brand/courtyard-ai-v1.jpg" alt="Generated fictional courtyard with terracotta walls and a green chair" width="1200" height="630" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>Generated demonstration artwork used by the photo pair. <a href="/demo">Open the composition explainer</a>.</figcaption></figure>
     <h3>The owner-only check</h3><p>On a composition you are authorised to manage, the fuller test is: submit a test photo, check that it is private, approve it, feature it, follow its source link, then hide it at the source. On the next public request, both controlled views should stop showing it. Ask the agent to verify the media URL too.</p><p>The public demo requires an invited owner credential for those writes. Browse it without an account; do not claim the moderation test from browsing alone. Downloaded copies cannot be recalled.</p>
     <details><summary>Why this is more than two links</summary><p>The showcase asks the guestbook for currently eligible items through a versioned contract. Source repositories and storage remain independent. Composer pins the code versions it runs together, while current source visibility governs public content. <a href="/architecture">See the architecture</a>.</p></details>
    </>}

    {lesson.id==='content'&&<>
     <p>When an app needs structured content, plan the fields people will use. A collection groups entries of one kind; fields describe each entry. EmDash provides those content concepts, while Astro supplies the public pages.</p>
     <h3>Try this optional branch</h3><ol><li>Open <a href="/init/content" target="_blank" rel="noopener">the content and form builder in another tab</a>.</li><li>Use a sample pattern, or design a small collection such as project notes: a title, a summary and a link.</li><li>Try the form with sample values. Check the required fields, help text and any conditions you chose.</li><li>Inspect the portable model and native EmDash seed export. Ask your agent to review them against the installed EmDash version before implementation.</li></ol>
     <h3>Check the boundary</h3><p>The builder lets you author and exercise a model locally. Its preview and seed export do not establish that a customer app has been built or deployed. The agent must implement the app, test its server-side rules and check the actual live form.</p>
     <details><summary>Coming from WordPress?</summary><p>Collections, fields, media and draft/published states should feel familiar. The page layer and plugin implementation differ. A familiar concept does not guarantee that a particular WordPress plugin can be replaced without review. <a href="https://docs.emdashcms.com/coming-from/wordpress/">Read the official EmDash concept map</a>.</p></details>
     <details><summary>When would a diagram help?</summary><p>Use a diagram when it makes a relationship clearer: who can publish a photo, where content lives, or which app supplies a card. Ask for editable source, a readable phone version and a text explanation. Installing a diagram tool is useful only when you can use the result.</p></details>
    </>}

    {lesson.id==='quality'&&<>
     <p>A good-looking preview is a start. People need to understand the next action, read the content and recover when something goes wrong. Check those things on the page they actually use.</p>
     <h3>Try this on your app</h3><ol><li>Open it on your phone, or at a narrow browser width. Read the heading and use the main action. Look for sideways scrolling, tiny text or controls covered by other content.</li><li>Use a keyboard: Tab to the controls, notice the focus indicator, and activate buttons and links. Try any form with incomplete input.</li><li>Read the page’s words aloud. Replace vague promises with the action and useful result. Verify factual claims before publishing them.</li><li>Ask another person to complete the main task without coaching. Note where they hesitate, then give the agent that specific problem.</li></ol>
     <div class="agent-request"><h3>Ask for a focused quality pass</h3><p data-agent-prompt="quality">Review my current app’s main user journey. Check readable phone and desktop layouts, keyboard operation, visible focus, contrast, reduced motion and useful error recovery. Inspect the rendered page and exercise its actual controls. Fix the smallest material problems while preserving its identity and existing work. Run relevant checks and follow the project’s own release rules. Report what you verified and any device or human check still needed.</p><button class="secondary" data-copy-prompt="quality" hidden><Copy size={16} aria-hidden="true"/>Copy quality request</button><p role="status" data-copy-status="quality"></p></div>
     <details><summary>How do I spot generic AI writing?</summary><p>Watch for promises that could describe any app, unnecessary slogans and claims with no source. Say what the user can do, use a concrete example and remove filler. A score from an AI detector cannot prove writing quality. Neither can having a design skill installed.</p></details>
     <details><summary>What can the agent verify automatically?</summary><p>It can inspect layout, run code checks, exercise supported controls and compare intended content with live output. Those help find defects. Your physical device, your taste and someone else’s understanding still need direct observation.</p></details>
    </>}

    {lesson.id==='own'&&<>
     <p>You should leave with an app you can find and a clear record of how it was released. Keep the source and configuration under your control. Ask the agent to leave recovery instructions that describe what has actually been tested.</p>
     <h3>Before you finish</h3><ol><li>Find your GitHub repository and app address. Ask which account owns each and which permissions are needed for future updates.</li><li>Keep the downloaded brief, relevant source revision and release evidence with the project. Store credentials through the project’s documented secret handling.</li><li>Ask what would happen if a release fails, a permission is revoked or you need to restore data. Separate a tested recovery path from a proposed one.</li><li>Choose your next small change, or stop here with your working starter. You can return to any lesson later.</li></ol>
     <h3>Code history and data are different</h3><p>Returning to an earlier code version must preserve current customisations and privacy. It should not make hidden photos public again. Data backup and restoration need their own evidence. General customer-source updates, cleanup and complete recovery remain open work; ask for the supported path for your particular app.</p>
     <h3>Backups recover data. Redundancy addresses downtime.</h3>
     <p>A backup gives you an earlier recoverable copy after deletion or corruption. Redundancy supplies another working path through a particular failure. Replicas or a mirror can repeat a mistaken deletion, and two deployments sharing the same account, database or provider can fail together. Mini-apps do not automatically provide either protection.</p>
     <p>Decide <strong>how much recent data you could lose</strong> and <strong>how long the app could be unavailable</strong>. A personal static page and an app collecting irreplaceable contributions need different recovery plans. These targets guide the work; a restore rehearsal tells you whether you can meet them.</p>
     <ul><li><strong>Source and configuration:</strong> retain committed code, dependency pins, owner customisations, destination settings and the matching release identity. Know how you would access these if the primary account became unavailable.</li><li><strong>Database:</strong> check the actual recovery window, export needs and migration procedure for the selected environment. D1 Time Travel can restore a database within its plan-specific window; it overwrites that database and does not restore uploaded media. <a href="https://developers.cloudflare.com/d1/reference/time-travel/">Check the current D1 recovery limits</a>.</li><li><strong>Media:</strong> preserve uploaded files as well as database references. Check representative file contents and a manifest against the backup. A copy that mirrors deletions without retaining a recoverable version is insufficient for deletion recovery.</li><li><strong>Access and keys:</strong> keep account recovery and required encryption keys in an appropriate protected store, separately from public source and exported app specifications. Without required keys, a data copy may be unusable.</li></ul>
     <p><strong>Before you rely on it:</strong> restore a permitted test dataset and files into an isolated environment, verify the main journey, and record what was recovered and how long it took. Choose retention and backup access that respect privacy; a restore must reconcile later deletions and revocations before anything is served publicly. <a href="https://docs.emdashcms.com/guides/backups/">EmDash’s recovery guide</a> distinguishes database, media and key recovery. <a href="https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/versions-and-deployments/rollbacks/">Worker rollback</a> restores code, not bound storage.</p>
     <details><summary>What is a proportionate first setup?</summary><p>For a small public site: keep source history, a repeatable release, checks of the main page and action, and a named update/recovery owner. When you collect valuable data, add protected recoverable database/media copies and a tested restore path. Consider a copy outside the production failure boundary when losing that account would also lose the backup.</p><p>Add a redundant deployment or failover only when the cost of downtime warrants it. Ask what exact failure it covers, how data stays consistent, how traffic switches and how you test the switch. More infrastructure adds cost and coordination. An alert can warn about usage; it is not a spending cap. Review actual provider costs before changing resources.</p></details>
     <div class="agent-request"><h3>Ask for an app-specific recovery assessment</h3><p data-agent-prompt="recovery">Inspect this app’s source, configuration, database, uploaded media, access and encryption-key dependencies. Explain what would be lost after a bad deploy, deletion, lost account or provider outage. Propose a proportionate backup/retention and recovery approach against my acceptable data loss and downtime; identify those targets if not decided. Separate configured protections from a successfully tested restore. Explain any redundancy benefit, shared failure points, maintenance and costs. Preserve current privacy and deletion decisions. Keep this assessment read-only; do not restore production, copy sensitive data, provision resources or add spend. Identify one bounded authorised restore rehearsal and the exact permissions it would require.</p><button class="secondary" data-copy-prompt="recovery" hidden><Copy size={16} aria-hidden="true"/>Copy recovery-assessment request</button><p role="status" data-copy-status="recovery"></p></div>
     <div class="agent-request"><h3>Ask for a useful handover</h3><p data-agent-prompt="own">Summarise this app’s actual state for its owner. Give the intended repository and live URL, verified source and deployed identity, configuration to preserve, supported update and recovery steps, and exact unresolved checks. Distinguish code rollback from data restoration and keep current privacy decisions intact. Keep credentials, private records and raw provider responses out of the summary. Recommend one small next improvement, or explain why the current working app is a good stopping point.</p><button class="secondary" data-copy-prompt="own" hidden><Copy size={16} aria-hidden="true"/>Copy handover request</button><p role="status" data-copy-status="own"></p></div>
     <p>Your observations below record what you personally checked. They do not certify an environment, deployment or recovery procedure. <a href="/release">Compare with current Composer release evidence</a>.</p>
    </>}

    <section class="lesson-observations" aria-labelledby={lesson.id+'-observations'}><h3 id={lesson.id+'-observations'}><Check size={20} aria-hidden="true"/>What did you observe?</h3><p class="small">Optional personal notes. Tick only what you saw; move on or revisit any lesson.</p>{checks[lesson.id as keyof typeof checks].map((text,checkIndex)=><label><input type="checkbox" data-observation={`${lesson.id}-${checkIndex}`} disabled/><span>{text}</span></label>)}</section>
    <details class="lesson-question"><summary>Check your understanding</summary><fieldset><legend>{questions[index].question}</legend>{questions[index].answers.map((answer,answerIndex)=><label><input type="radio" name={'question-'+lesson.id} value={answerIndex} data-answer data-correct={questions[index].correct} data-explanation={questions[index].why} disabled/><span>{answer}</span></label>)}<p data-answer-feedback role="status" hidden></p></fieldset><noscript><p>{questions[index].answers[questions[index].correct]} {questions[index].why}</p></noscript></details>
    <div class="lesson-navigation">{index>0?<a class="button secondary" href={'#'+lessons[index-1].id}><ArrowLeft size={16} aria-hidden="true"/>Previous lesson</a>:<a href="/ready/guide" class="setup-link"><BookOpen size={16} aria-hidden="true"/>Detailed setup help</a>}{index<lessons.length-1?<a class="button" href={'#'+lessons[index+1].id}>Next: {lessons[index+1].title}<ArrowRight size={16} aria-hidden="true"/></a>:<a class="button" href="/init">Choose your next app <ArrowRight size={16} aria-hidden="true"/></a>}</div>
   </article>)}
